      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}-3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{6}+3.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{6}+3,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}+3}{\\sqrt{6}+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{6}+3)}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}-(3)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{18}+3\\sqrt{3}}{6-9} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}+3\\sqrt{3}}{-3} & = \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
